<h1 class="ct-art-title">Content syndication in streaming marketing: the Netflix case study</h1>
<p class="ct-art-dek">How four localised assets a week grew Netflix Nigeria on Instagram from under 10K to 600K+ followers.</p>
<div class="ct-art-meta"><img alt="" src="/img/team-subomi.webp"/><span>Olasubomi Archibong</span><span class="dot"></span><span>6 min read</span></div>
</div>
<img alt="Netflix Nigeria social content engine, 2020 to 2024: from under 10K to 600K+ Instagram followers" class="ct-art-cover" src="/img/work-netflix-nigeria.webp"/>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section ct-art-body">
<div class="container w-layout-blockcontainer">
<div class="ct-art-col">
<div class="ct-art-stats">
<div><b>600K+</b><span>Instagram followers, up from under 10K</span></div>
<div><b>220K</b><span>Paid subscribers in Nigeria, up from about 40K</span></div>
<div><b>1M+</b><span>Followers across all platforms</span></div>
<div><b>50+</b><span>Titles promoted, Nigerian and global</span></div>
</div>
<p class="ct-art-lead">Between 2020 and 2024, Netflix was releasing a steady wave of Nigerian and international titles. Each one had to feel relevant, timely and worth talking about, on four platforms with different rules. We built the engine that made that happen every week.</p>
<h2>The challenge</h2>
<p>A streaming catalogue is a content marketer's dream and a planning headache. There is always something new, and there is always something older worth reviving. The risk is repetition fatigue: a feed that starts to feel templated, mechanical or overly promotional. We needed structure without losing freshness, across three pressures at once.</p>
<ol class="ct-art-list">
<li><b>Cultural localisation.</b> Global titles had to feel local without bending the Netflix brand.</li>
<li><b>Platform adaptation.</b> Instagram, Facebook, TikTok and X each reward a different format and rhythm.</li>
<li><b>Sustained cadence.</b> Attention had to hold between major releases, not just during launch weeks.</li>
</ol>
<h2>The approach</h2>
<p>Every title started with two questions: what about this story resonates with Nigerian audiences, and how should that show up, visually and in tone, on each platform? Every long-form release was then broken down into trailers, key art, reels and scene carousels, and fed back into a shared calendar. That cycle rests on three pillars.</p>
<h3>1. Localisation as strategy</h3>
<p>For global hits, localisation meant speaking the way people actually speak. Money Heist was recut with a Pidgin dub for Nigerian fans, and Jingle Jangle opened with "This na di tory of Jeronicus Jangle." For Nigerian originals the job flipped: lean into pride and introduce the cast the way a fan would, one character card at a time, so a Yoruba epic like Lísàbí felt familiar before episode one.</p>
<div class="ct-art-figs two"><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Woman Crush Wednesday spotlight post" loading="lazy" src="/img/nf-wcw.webp"/><figcaption>A Woman Crush Wednesday spotlight, built around a local cast member.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Quote card from a key scene" loading="lazy" src="/img/nf-quote-card.webp"/><figcaption>Quote cards lifted the lines fans were already repeating.</figcaption></figure></div>
<h3>2. Platform-native adaptation</h3>
<p>We never resized one asset four ways. Instagram got structured carousels built for saves and swipes, TikTok got faster edits on trending audio, X got conversational hooks and Facebook got context-led captions. The format was the idea: Hijack '93 became passport cards and a "Who's flying?" pick-your-character grid, and Òlòtūré became a swipeable fact file.</p>
<div class="ct-art-figs three"><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Hijack 93 passport card for Captain Ambrose" loading="lazy" src="/img/nf-hijack-passport.webp"/><figcaption>Hijack ’93: passport cards for each character.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Who is flying pick-your-character grid" loading="lazy" src="/img/nf-whos-flying.webp"/><figcaption>A “Who’s flying?” pick that invited replies.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Òlòtūré behind-the-scenes fact carousel cover" loading="lazy" src="/img/netflix-oloture.webp"/><figcaption>Òlòtūré: The Journey as a fact carousel.</figcaption></figure></div>
<h3>3. A continuous content engine</h3>
<p>Instead of campaign bursts around each release, a shared calendar with Netflix's team balanced new titles with revived catalogue, sequenced themes so they did not overlap, and left room for reactive moments. Monthly roundups, weekly slates, meme edits and brand moments kept the account publishing four new assets a week without fatigue.</p>
<div class="ct-art-figs two"><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="What We Watched monthly roundup for October" loading="lazy" src="/img/netflix-what-we-watched.webp"/><figcaption>What We Watched: the monthly roundup.</figcaption></figure><figure class="ct-art-fig"><img alt="Now Playing weekly slate" loading="lazy" src="/img/nf-now-playing.webp"/><figcaption>Now Playing: the week’s new slate.</figcaption></figure></div>
<blockquote class="ct-art-quote">The format was the idea, not a resized poster.</blockquote>
<h2>The results</h2>
<p>Four localised assets a week built an audience that kept coming back to the catalogue. Instagram grew more than 60 times, from under 10K to over 600K followers, and the account passed 1M followers across platforms. During our engagement, paid subscribers in Nigeria grew from about 40K to 220K. Other factors moved that number too, but the content kept Netflix part of the conversation every single week.</p>
<div class="ct-art-results">
<div><span>Instagram followers</span><b>Under 10K → 600K+</b><i style="--w:100%"></i></div>
<div><span>Paid subscribers in Nigeria</span><b>About 40K → 220K</b><i style="--w:37%"></i></div>
<div><span>Average per post</span><b>10K views · 1K likes</b><i style="--w:18%"></i></div>
</div>
<h2>What any catalogue brand can take from this</h2>
<ul class="ct-art-list">
<li><b>Localise the meaning, not just the language.</b> Ask what makes the story matter to this audience before you design anything.</li>
<li><b>Let the platform shape the format.</b> One idea, many native expressions beats one asset everywhere.</li>
<li><b>Plan for the weeks between launches.</b> A calendar that revives the back catalogue is what keeps attention compounding.</li>
</ul>
